et to that number of unemployed persons  per job opening is falling that is a  good sign it's .7 today but it was  not so hot just a few months ago so that  is improving which is a good sign but  still we continue to see that the  unemployment rate is at 4.6 there are  10.4 million open jobs and 4.4 million  people in the month of september quit  their jobs and gave the middle finger to  their employer so we got two quotes here  the first quote coming from nick bunker  the economic research director at indeed  the rise of quitting across the labor  market is remarkable but the  concentration among a few sectors is eye  popping quits are up the most in sectors  where most work is in person or  relatively low paying you want to make  me go to the office people are quitting  you want to pay me not that much money  people are quitting doesn't take a  genius to try to figure this out then we  also have this quote from ben ires who's  a senior economist at nationwide who  said we do expect that higher  compensation and plentiful openings will  draw more workers to re-enter the labor  force in coming months hoping to ease  the tight labor market somewhat but as  the unemployment rate approaches  pre-coveted levels next year and is  expected to drop further beyond that the  labor market could remain relatively  tight throughout the current expansion  now here is my take on all of this all  this data is amazing but guess what  there's three things that people should  take away from this one is that  inflation is at over six percent people  quite literally are falling behind if  i'm going to work every day and i'm  falling behind i can't stay there i at  some point have to say i got to do  something different so they quit their  job and they go find either a different  job or they go try to figure something  else out so inflation drives massive  massive pain for people who cannot  continue to get raises that keep up with  the rate of inflation so for you at home  go ask your 